encyclopedia :esvar do
  name "Esvar"
  summary "Esvar is a Tessellan practice of divination through pictured mantle offices and changing ringglass cards, used in private readings and public contests over a foretold future."
  kind :culture
  subkind :belief
  status :complete
  topics :religion, :cosmology, :resonance
  prevalence :uncommon
  available_globally

  prose <<~PROSE
    An esvar reader lays out pictured cards around the figures of the #{encyclopedia_ref :the_three_forms, "Three Forms"}. People, beasts, doors and empty seats surround the mantle offices. Position matters: a figure beneath a crown can be its subject, its support or the person about to take it. Readers ask the person seeking counsel to move the card they fear most. A reading includes that choice and the arrangement it leaves behind.

    Painted decks belong to households, gambling rooms and devotional houses. Ringglass decks are rarer. Their figures are cut into thin crystal leaves, and light sometimes draws additional strokes beneath the engraved picture. These changes can remain visible after the reader has gone. Esvar names the practice; an esvar deck is its instrument, and an esvar reading is one particular interpretation. An unchanging painted deck can support the practice without producing a foretelling.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :resonance, heading: "An Office Occupied"
    Mantle figures answer changes beyond the room containing the cards. The distant recordings compared after #{ref :somas_last_road, "Soma's Last Road"} show the road divided beneath two silhouettes during the succession, before news of the rescue arrived. That evidence has made divided roads a sign readers watch for even when they know nothing of the bearer.

    Such responses give the offices a significance beyond the bearer's territory. A transfer changes the figure through which distant readers encounter Form, Will and the disputed third panel. Some keepers understand this as a person entering an existing divine office; others describe a human life becoming part of a resonance pattern that several worlds can answer. Neither account establishes who, if anyone, chooses the bearer. A captain can change those figures while fighting for a single city.

    Qualification and possession remain distinguishable. Nalis had opened an unfamiliar destination before Soma yielded the occupied road. Eris had prepared Ruvian's witnesses before its restorer disclosed her loss. Repeating a qualifying act has never provided a reliable way to summon either mantle, and painted crowns confer no authority.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "Warnings That Can Be Answered"
    A changing card supplies an image without a date or explanation. Portraits can be unrecognizable; a rescuer and an executioner can carry the same knife. Readers preserve the image separately from their interpretation. The #{ref :talessar, "Talessar"} deck has shown a particular structure before it fell and people who were absent when it did. Its history is cited both by those who trust foretellings and by those who insist that a warning demands intervention.

    Decks do not answer questions on demand. Most readings produce no new stroke. Rearranging a spread can suggest another reading but has not been shown to change an event by itself. Buyers pay heavily for a leaf that seems to name them; bearers and would-be bearers have stolen cards, sponsored readers and destroyed unfavorable copies. No destruction has been shown to erase the danger pictured.
  PROSE

  cue "A reader asks a visitor to move the card they fear most; another person at the table reaches to stop their hand."
  cue "Light reveals a stroke below the surface of a ringglass card; the reader turns the leaf over to see which engraved figure had hidden it."
  affordance "An unchanged drawing of an earlier foretelling can reveal which details a celebrated reader added after the event."
  pressure "A mantle claimant wants a reading performed publicly and offers protection to everyone willing to stand beside the chosen card."
  variation "Painted household decks tell through arrangement and interpretation; ringglass leaves sometimes acquire strokes nobody cut."
  variation "Some Communion houses sound the lawful figures during readings; others use unpowered cards and refuse to sound the third panel."
end
